\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
% \lipsum[1]
In this paper, we explore a simple yet effective \emph{training and optimization-free approach} for improving 
both single and multi-subject consistency for visual storytelling.
% text-to-image consistency across multiple characters. 
We note that prior works often struggle when scaling to multiple characters and can have visible inconsistencies in finegrain character details. To address this, we first analyse the reason for these limitations. Our exploration reveals that the primary-issue stems from \emph{self-attention leakage}, which is exacerbated when trying to ensure consistency across multiple-characters. Motivated by these findings, we next a propose a region-based planning and storyboard generation approach which uses \emph{bounded cross-frame self-attention}  for reducing inter-character leakage and  \emph{cross-frame token merging} for aligning fine-grain character features. Despite its simplicity and training-free nature, we observe that the proposed approach surpasses prior \emph{state-of-art} in terms of both character consistency and text-to-image alignment while exhibiting $\times 30$ faster inference speed than optimization-based methods.
% (Dreambooth-Lora \cite{ruiz2022dreambooth}, TI \cite{gal2022image}).
We hope that our research can help open new avenues for the development of consistent multi-character visual storytelling.


