\begin{abstract}
While multimodal systems have achieved impressive advances, they typically rely on text-aligned representations rather than directly integrating audio and visual inputs. This reliance can limit the use of acoustic information in tasks requiring nuanced audio understanding. In response, \textit{SoundCLIP} explores direct audio-visual integration within multimodal large language models (MLLMs) by substituting CLIP’s visual tokens with audio representations and selecting sound-relevant patch tokens in models such as Large Language and Vision Assistant (LLaVA). We investigate two configurations: (1) projecting audio features into CLIP’s visual manifold via a multilayer perceptron trained with InfoNCE on paired audio-video segments, and (2) preserving raw audio embeddings with minimal dimensional adjustments. Experiments with five state-of-the-art audio encoders reveal a fundamental trade-off. While audio-to-video retrieval performance increases dramatically (up to 44 percentage points in Top-1 accuracy) when audio is projected into CLIP’s space, text generation quality declines: BLEU and ROUGE-L scores drop by 0.024, BERTScore by 0.057, and GPT-4\textsubscript{o} evaluations by 0.13--0.37 points. Encoders pre-trained with text supervision (CLAP, Whisper, ImageBind) maintain stronger generative capabilities than those focused primarily on audiovisual alignment (Wav2CLIP, AudioCLIP), highlighting the value of language exposure for generation tasks. We introduce \textit{WhisperCLIP}, an architecture that fuses intermediate representations from Whisper, as well as \textit{AudioVisual Event Evaluation (AVE-2)}, a dataset of 580{,}147 three-second audiovisual clips with fine-grained alignment annotations. Our findings challenge the assumption that stronger cross-modal alignment necessarily benefits all multimodal tasks; instead, a Pareto frontier emerges wherein optimal performance depends on balancing retrieval accuracy with text generation quality. The codes and datasets can be accessed via \url{https://github.com/ali-vosoughi/SoundCLIP}.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Despite remarkable advances in multimodal AI, a critical limitation is that most existing systems rely on text as an intermediary for cross-modal tasks. This text-centric paradigm—exemplified by CLIP \citep{radford2021learning} for image-text and CLAP \citep{elizalde2023clap} for audio-text—creates an information bottleneck, especially for modalities like audio where rich acoustic cues cannot be captured by textual descriptions. To address this limitation, we introduce and rigorously evaluate a direct audio-visual training approach that bypasses text-mediated alignment, leveraging off-the-shelf pre-trained audio encoders to facilitate robust audio-visual binding without discarding critical acoustic information. This strategy has significant implications for the development of multimodal language models capable of handling diverse data sources.

A key scientific contribution of this work is the discovery and quantification of a previously unrecognized trade-off in cross-modal representation learning between retrieval performance and generation quality. Through controlled experiments using multiple audio encoders and benchmarks, we present the first empirical evidence challenging the widespread assumption that stronger cross-modal alignment unilaterally benefits multimodal systems. These findings advance our conceptual understanding of representation learning and carry broad ramifications for multimodal AI research.

Our proposed framework, \frameworkname{}, enables a training-free integration of audio features into CLIP-based language models by employing a principled token substitution scheme. Unlike prior approaches that either discard modality-specific information or mandate extensive architectural overhauls, \frameworkname{} retains the rich acoustic attributes required for nuanced comprehension while preserving architectural compatibility with existing vision-language models. This method aligns with recent theoretical insights on embedding space geometry \citep{li2021align} and also delivers practical innovations for multimodal integration.

Comprehensive evaluation across five state-of-the-art audio encoders—ImageBind \citep{girdhar2023imagebind}, Whisper \citep{radford2023whisper}, CLAP \citep{elizalde2023clap}, AudioCLIP \citep{guzhov2022audioclip}, and Wav2CLIP \citep{wu2022wav2clip}—on four diverse benchmarks reveals that audio encoders exhibit dramatically different CLIP-compatibility, with projection improving Top-1 audio-to-video retrieval by up to 44.4 percentage points. We further observe a systematic inverse relationship between retrieval performance and generation quality, as evidenced by GPT-4\textsubscript{o} evaluation scores decreasing by 0.13--0.37 points for more tightly aligned representations. Moreover, encoders with prior text exposure demonstrate superior generation capabilities even when alignment occurs without text, suggesting essential inductive biases for cross-modal reasoning.

To encourage further progress, we introduce two key resources. The first is \proposedaudioencoder{}, a method that fuses intermediate representations from Whisper to better reconcile the retrieval-generation trade-off. The second is \proposeddataset{}, a carefully curated dataset of 580{,}145 audio-visual clips with fine-grained alignment annotations spanning five quality dimensions. Both resources will be publicly released to spur more extensive investigation into this newly identified trade-off.

Our findings establish a Pareto frontier in cross-modal representation learning, underscoring that task-specific optimization is crucial. While tighter alignment with CLIP’s visual manifold benefits retrieval tasks, generation tasks require preserving richer modality-specific detail. This work thus advances both the theoretical understanding of cross-modal representations and offers practical guidance for designing multimodal AI systems that can flexibly and effectively handle diverse modalities while maintaining their unique informational strengths.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. We first introduce the \textit{AVE-2} dataset, describing its design principles and quality control mechanisms. We then present the \textit{SoundCLIP} framework, including the mathematical formulation of cross-modal alignment and the WhisperCLIP architecture. Next, we detail the experimental results that quantify the retrieval-generation trade-off. We position our findings in the context of related research before concluding with broader implications and directions for future work.


\section{AudioVisual Event Evaluation (AVE-2) Dataset}
\label{sec:dataset}

Effective study of audio-visual interactions requires precisely controlled alignment data. Existing datasets (AudioSet \citep{audioset}, VGG-Sound \citep{chen2020vggsound}, Kinetics \citep{kay2017kinetics}) lack detailed alignment annotations or acoustic diversity. We introduce AVE-2, a dataset specifically designed for cross-modal alignment research.

