\subsection{Dataset Design}
\label{subsec:dataset_design}
AVE-2 comprises 580,147 video segments (3 seconds each), evaluated through our five-dimensional alignment scoring: \textbf{Temporal Alignment} (mean: 7.54), \textbf{Spatial Coherence} (8.35), \textbf{Contextual Relevance} (6.32), \textbf{Physical Causality} (9.02), and \textbf{Sound Source Visibility} (8.04), following the prompts described in \citep{vosoughi2025quality}. These dimensions were selected to capture fundamental aspects of audio-visual perception, from temporal synchronization to semantic congruency. Score distributions vary meaningfully across dimensions, with physical causality showing high consistency (99.71\% of visible source segments score >8.0) while temporal alignment exhibits greater variability.

\subsection{Dataset Composition}
\label{subsec:dataset_composition}
The dataset balances visible sources (490,546 segments), invisible sources (337,069), and silent objects (446,567) across diverse categories. This three-way categorization creates a more comprehensive evaluation benchmark than prior datasets, which predominantly feature only visible sound sources. The content spans 157,800 unique visible source categories, 111,644 invisible source categories, and 300,265 silent object categories, with distinctive frequency patterns (human subjects dominate visible sources, ambient sounds prevail in invisible sources, environmental elements in silent objects).

\subsection{Alignment Evaluation Framework}
\label{subsec:alignment_framework}
AVE-2's five-dimensional framework enables: (1) analysis of which alignment aspects most affect cross-modal integration; (2) targeted filtering for specialized experiments; (3) granular quality control; and (4) creation of task-optimized subsets. Our annotation process achieved high inter-annotator agreement (Cohen's κ > 0.82) across all dimensions.

AVE-2 supports various applications including cross-modal alignment research, sound source localization, and multimodal scene understanding. The complete dataset, with alignment scores and annotation tools, will be publicly released.


\section{SoundCLIP Framework}
\label{sec:method}

We formulate the problem of audio integration into vision-language models as a representation alignment challenge: can audio embeddings effectively substitute for visual tokens without architectural modifications to the underlying LLM? Our solution, \textbf{SoundCLIP}, enables direct token-level substitution through a principled manifold alignment approach, treating audio tokens as if they were visual tokens within CLIP's embedding space.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figs/soundclip-architecture.pdf}
    \caption{\textbf{SoundCLIP Architecture.} Our framework performs training-free audio-to-vision integration through: (1) encoding audio using pretrained models, (2) aligning representations with CLIP's visual space via a lightweight MLP (1.9M params) or preserving raw embeddings, and (3) substituting the CLIP [CLS] token while selecting top-k similar visual patches based on cosine similarity.}
    \label{fig:framework_overview}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Modal Manifold Alignment}
\label{subsec:method:manifold}

Building on geometric approaches to cross-modal learning \citep{li2021align}, we formulate audio-visual token substitution as an embedding space alignment problem. Given that vision-language models like LLaVA expect inputs from CLIP's visual manifold, we must either transform audio representations to match this distribution or adapt the model to handle audio's native geometric properties.

\paragraph{Problem Formulation.}
Let an audiovisual segment $\mathcal{C} = (\mathbf{a}, \mathbf{v})$ consist of audio waveform $\mathbf{a} \in \mathbb{R}^{T_a}$ and visual frames $\mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^{T_v \times H \times W \times 3}$. A frozen audio encoder $\phi_a$ produces embedding $\mathbf{h}_a = \phi_a(\mathbf{a}) \in \mathbb{R}^{d_a}$, while a frozen CLIP ViT-L/14 encoder $\phi_v$ yields $\mathbf{V} = \phi_v(\mathbf{v}) \in \mathbb{R}^{(N+1)\times 1024}$, whose first row $\mathbf{c}$ is the global \texttt{[CLS]} token and the remaining $N\!=\!576$ rows are patch tokens $\{\mathbf{v}_i\}_{i=1}^N$. Our objective is to learn a mapping $\mathcal{M}: \mathbb{R}^{d_a} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{1024}$ such that $\mathbf{f}_a = \mathcal{M}(\mathbf{h}_a)$ can effectively substitute for visual tokens in the downstream language model while preserving relevant acoustic information.

\paragraph{Alignment Strategies.}
We investigate two complementary approaches for audio-visual integration that represent opposite priorities: (i) \textbf{CLIP-Aligned (Proj-MLP)}: A manifold projection module transforms audio into CLIP's visual space using a three-block MLP structure with 1.9M parameters; (ii) \textbf{Non-Aligned (Raw-Pad)}: A dimensionality-matching approach that preserves the native audio embedding distribution through simple padding or truncation. This parameter-free approach serves both as a baseline and as an alternative strategy that prioritizes preserving modality-specific information.

