To maximize the effectiveness of audio token substitution, we introduce WhisperCLIP, a novel audio encoder that leverages Whisper's rich acoustic representations while addressing its speech-centric bias. Through systematic analysis of Whisper's internal representations, we discovered that higher layers progressively filter out non-speech audio information—a beneficial property for ASR but detrimental for general acoustic understanding.

\paragraph{Multi-Layer Fusion.}
Given Whisper's layer-wise hidden states $\mathbf{H}=\{\mathbf{H}^{(l)}\}_{l=1}^{L}$ where $\mathbf{H}^{(l)} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times S \times d_w}$, we explore multiple strategies to extract audio representations that balance speech understanding with general acoustic information. Our comprehensive ablations revealed that the \emph{average-all} variant offers the best performance: $\mathbf{z}_a = \frac{1}{L}\sum_{i=1}^{L}\text{MeanTime}(\mathbf{H}^{(i)})$, where $\text{MeanTime}(\cdot)$ performs temporal averaging across sequence positions. This approach achieves 8.2pp higher Top-1 retrieval (lower rank) accuracy compared to using only the final layer.

\subsection{Token-Level Integration and Training-Free LLM Adaptation}
\label{subsec:method:token}
SoundCLIP enables training-free audio integration with vision-language models through selective token substitution. Our approach (Algorithm \ref{alg:soundclip} in Appendix) performs four operations: (1) audio representation extraction, (2) manifold alignment, (3) CLS token replacement, and (4) similarity-guided patch selection retaining the $k$ most relevant visual patches.

The transformed representation $\mathbf{V}^{\star} \in \mathbb{R}^{(N+1)\times 1024}$ feeds directly into LLaVA's vision pathway with no additional training, offering practical advantages: minimal overhead (1.9M parameters, 0.8ms latency, <2MB VRAM) and compatibility with any CLIP-based model.

Our WhisperCLIP layer fusion approach (Figure \ref{fig:whisperclip_layers}) preserves acoustic information from intermediate layers that would otherwise be filtered out, balancing speech content with environmental sounds and musical elements.

This investigation reveals a fundamental trade-off: CLIP-aligned embeddings improve retrieval performance (+44pp) but reduce generation quality. This confirms our theoretical model where output quality is proportional to $I_{shared} + \alpha I_{unique}$, with different tasks weighting these components differently. This discovery has significant implications for multimodal system design, suggesting that optimal architectures depend on whether the primary objective is retrieval or generation.
\section{Experimental Analysis}
\label{sec:experiments}

To systematically investigate the retrieval-generation trade-off in audio-visual representation learning, we conducted extensive experiments spanning multiple audio encoders, datasets, and evaluation protocols. Our experimental design specifically targets two key questions: (1) How effectively can different audio encoders be aligned with CLIP's visual space? and (2) What are the implications of such alignment for downstream tasks?

