Our cross-modal retrieval experiments reveal several key insights about audio-visual compatibility. As shown in Figure \ref{fig:retrieval_improvement}, projecting audio representations into CLIP's visual space yields remarkable improvements in retrieval performance across all encoders, with mean ranks improving by 4-10× on average.

\paragraph{Dramatic Improvement Through Alignment.} 
Our results demonstrate that audio-visual alignment via projection is extremely effective, with Top-1 accuracy improving by up to +44.4 percentage points (ImageBind on AudioCaps). This dramatic enhancement suggests that the fundamental barrier to audio-visual integration in CLIP-based language models is primarily representational misalignment rather than semantic incompatibility.

\paragraph{Encoder-Specific Alignment Capacity.} 
While all encoders benefit from CLIP alignment, their improvement magnitudes differ substantially. ImageBind exhibits exceptional compatibility with CLIP's visual space (mean rank improves from 50.23 to 4.78), while language-aligned encoders like CLAP show more modest improvements (53.94 to 13.61). This differential alignment capacity reveals that audio encoders pretrained with visual supervision inherit latent representational structures more compatible with CLIP's visual manifold.

\begin{tcolorbox}[colframe=black,
arc=2pt,
boxsep=-0.35em,
left=8pt,right=8pt,
]
\paragraph{\textbf{Finding 2.}} Our WhisperCLIP multi-layer fusion approach provides significant retrieval improvements over standard Whisper (+3.4pp to +4.9pp across datasets), confirming that intermediate Whisper layers preserve valuable non-speech acoustic information that enhances cross-modal alignment capabilities.
\end{tcolorbox}

\paragraph{WhisperCLIP's Enhanced Integration.} 
Our proposed WhisperCLIP architecture improves retrieval performance over standard Whisper by 3.4-4.9 percentage points across datasets through its multi-layer fusion approach. This confirms our hypothesis that standard Whisper progressively discards non-speech acoustic information in higher layers, and that reintroducing intermediate-layer information enhances cross-modal alignment capacity.

\paragraph{Symmetrical Bidirectional Performance.} 
The relative performance patterns remain consistent across both Audio→Video and Video→Audio retrieval directions (see Table \ref{tab:retrieval_audiocaps}), suggesting that our projection approach successfully creates a genuinely shared embedding space rather than a one-sided mapping. This bidirectional compatibility is crucial for general-purpose multimodal reasoning.

\subsection{Generation Quality Analysis}
\label{subsec:generation_results}

\begin{tcolorbox}[colframe=black,
arc=2pt,
boxsep=-0.35em,
left=8pt,right=8pt,
]
\paragraph{\textbf{Finding 3.}} CLIP alignment consistently degrades text generation quality across all encoders, with Whisper showing the most severe reduction in NLP metrics (BLEU -0.024, ROUGE-L -0.026, BERTScore -0.056) and GPT-4o evaluation scores (-0.37).
\end{tcolorbox}

