\paragraph{Comprehensive Evaluation Dimensions.} 
Figure \ref{fig:generation_dimensions} breaks down the quality decrease across different evaluation dimensions for the ImageBind encoder. CLIP alignment compromises performance across all aspects, but most severely affects Audio Quality (-14.0\%) and Reasoning (-20.4\%). This suggests that alignment sacrifices both low-level acoustic fidelity and higher-level semantic understanding, indicating a fundamental information loss during projection.

\paragraph{Training Paradigm Influence.} 
A notable pattern emerges when examining encoders by their training paradigm. Models trained with text involvement (CLAP, Whisper, ImageBind) demonstrate superior generation capabilities in their raw form, achieving higher GPT-4o scores (1.92, 1.87, and 1.89 respectively) compared to encoders focused solely on audiovisual alignment (AudioCLIP: 1.81, Wav2CLIP: 1.80). This suggests that exposure to language representations during pretraining creates beneficial inductive biases for generation tasks, even when text is not explicitly used during audio-visual integration.

\begin{tcolorbox}[colframe=black,
arc=2pt,
boxsep=-0.35em,
left=8pt,right=8pt,
]
\paragraph{\textbf{Finding 5.}} WhisperCLIP achieves the best generation performance of all encoders in both raw form (BLEU 0.103, BERTScore 0.271, GPT-4o 1.93) and CLIP-aligned form (BLEU 0.078, BERTScore 0.219, GPT-4o 1.58), demonstrating the effectiveness of multi-layer fusion for preserving acoustically rich representations.
\end{tcolorbox}

\paragraph{WhisperCLIP Advantage.} 
Our WhisperCLIP model achieves the best generation performance in both raw and aligned variants, outperforming all other encoders including standard Whisper. This demonstrates that our multi-layer fusion approach effectively preserves acoustic information beneficial for generation, supporting our theoretical motivation for the architecture.

\paragraph{Token Budget Impact.} 
Additional experiments indicate that increasing the token budget from $k=15$ to $k=150$ improves generation quality for both raw (+15.7\%) and aligned (+16.4\%) embeddings, but does not eliminate the performance gap between them. This confirms that the quality degradation from CLIP alignment cannot be mitigated simply by providing more visual context.

\subsection{Mechanistic Analysis of the Retrieval-Generation Trade-off}
\label{subsec:tradeoff_analysis}

\begin{tcolorbox}[colframe=black,
arc=2pt,
boxsep=-0.35em,
left=8pt,right=8pt,
]
\paragraph{\textbf{Finding 6.}} We identify a fundamental trade-off in cross-modal alignment: stronger retrieval improvement correlates strongly with greater generation quality loss (r=0.89), with encoders showing variable efficiency in managing this trade-off (from 0.05\% to 0.65\% quality loss per percentage point of retrieval gain).
\end{tcolorbox}

\begin{wrapfigure}[18]{r}{0.5\textwidth}
    \centering
    % \vspace{-10pt}
    \includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{figs/retrieval_generation_tradeoff.png}
    % \vspace{-10pt}
    \caption{\textbf{The fundamental retrieval-generation trade-off.} This visualization quantifies the relationship between retrieval improvement (x-axis) and generation quality degradation (y-axis) across all encoders. The strong positive correlation (r=0.89) confirms this is a systematic property of cross-modal alignment.}
    \label{fig:retrieval_generation_tradeoff}
\end{wrapfigure}


To understand the mechanism behind the retrieval-generation trade-off, we conducted a systematic analysis of embedding distributions, information content, and performance correlations.

Figure \ref{fig:retrieval_generation_tradeoff} visualizes the relationship between retrieval improvement and generation quality reduction across all encoders. The strong positive correlation (r=0.89) confirms that this trade-off is a systematic property of cross-modal alignment rather than an implementation artifact. Notably, encoders with larger retrieval gains (ImageBind, WhisperCLIP) exhibit more severe generation degradation, suggesting that stronger alignment comes at a higher cost to generative capacity.

\paragraph{Information-Theoretic Perspective.} 
We hypothesize that this trade-off arises from an information bottleneck effect during projection: aligning audio to CLIP's visual manifold requires compressing acoustic information that doesn't correspond to visual patterns, thereby discarding details crucial for nuanced generation. This is supported by the observation that semantic similarity (BERTScore) decreases more severely (-23.9\%) than surface-level metrics like ROUGE-L (-8.1\%) when using aligned embeddings.

\paragraph{Encoder-Specific Trade-off Efficiency.} 
Different encoders exhibit varying trade-off efficiencies, quantified as generation quality loss per percentage point of retrieval gain. Wav2CLIP shows the most favorable ratio (0.05\% per point), while CLAP exhibits the highest cost (0.65\% per point). This variation suggests that certain architectural properties can mitigate—but not eliminate—the fundamental tension between alignment and preservation.

\paragraph{Linear Interpolation Analysis.} 
Additional experiments with linear interpolation between raw and aligned embeddings failed to identify a middle ground that preserves both retrieval and generation performance, confirming that the trade-off represents a fundamental limitation rather than a simple optimization challenge.

\subsection{Practical Implications}
\label{subsec:practical_implications}

\begin{tcolorbox}[colframe=black,
arc=2pt,
boxsep=-0.35em,
left=8pt,right=8pt,
]
\paragraph{\textbf{Finding 7.}} This work establishes a Pareto frontier in cross-modal integration: applications prioritizing retrieval should use ImageBind with CLIP-aligned projections, while those requiring rich generation should use WhisperCLIP with raw embeddings and higher token budgets.
\end{tcolorbox}

Our comprehensive analysis yields several practical insights for deploying audio-visual token substitution in real-world applications:

\paragraph{Task-Optimized Configuration.} 
We provide evidence-based configuration guidelines for different application scenarios:
\begin{wrapfigure}{r}{0.65\textwidth}
\centering
\resizebox{0.64\textwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Application} & \textbf{Encoder} & \textbf{Alignment} & \textbf{Token Budget} \\
\midrule
Cross-modal retrieval & ImageBind & CLIP-aligned & - \\
Audio-visual search & ImageBind & CLIP-aligned & - \\
Audio question answering & WhisperCLIP & Raw & 150 \\
Dense captioning & WhisperCLIP & Raw & 150 \\
General understanding & WhisperCLIP & Raw & 150 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\caption{\textbf{Optimized configurations} for different audio-visual applications.}
\label{tab:optimized_config}
\end{wrapfigure}

SoundCLIP offers remarkable parameter efficiency, with the entire projection network requiring only 1.9M parameters—0.02\% of LLaVA-1.6's size. The processing overhead is minimal (0.8ms per clip, <2MB VRAM), making our approach viable even for resource-constrained deployments. The ability to select token budgets provides additional flexibility for balancing performance against computational requirements.
\paragraph{Implementation Efficiency.} 
SoundCLIP is designed to be resource efficient, using merely 1.9M parameters, achieving a latency of 0.8ms, and using less than 2MB of VRAM, which accounts for only 0.02\% of the total capacity of LLaVA-1.6. The token budgets are adjustable, allowing for adaptable resource management in deployments with limited resources.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_works}

Our work bridges four research areas in multimodal learning. In audio-visual encoding, large-scale multimodal pretraining has shown remarkable progress through models like CLIP~\citep{radford2021learning} for image-text, CLAP~\citep{elizalde2023clap} for audio-text, and ImageBind~\citep{girdhar2023imagebind} for multi-domain alignment. These approaches typically use text as an intermediary anchor, creating independent embedding spaces with limited cross-modal integration. Research on text-free audio-visual learning~\citep{hamilton2024separating, arandjelovic2023objectsounds} has demonstrated the value of natural correspondences in preserving acoustic details, though challenges remain in handling temporal misalignment and off-screen sounds, which our AVE-2 dataset specifically addresses. Recent multimodal LLMs~\citep{liu2023visual, li2023blip} predominantly process visual inputs through frozen CLIP encoders projected into language space, while audio integration lags behind, typically reduced to text via ASR~\citep{radford2023whisper}, discarding rich acoustic information. Unlike approaches that rely on textual pivoting~\citep{nguyen2024oscar, vosoughi2024learning, bi2024eagle} or image-centric intermediaries~\citep{su2024from}, SoundCLIP offers direct alignment of audio with CLIP's visual token space through a lightweight projection module (1.9M parameters) that requires no architectural modifications to the underlying language model. Our systematic analysis of the retrieval-generation trade-off provides the first quantitative evidence challenging the assumption that stronger cross-modal alignment universally benefits multimodal systems.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

SoundCLIP reveals a fundamental trade-off in cross-modal representation learning: MLP projection onto CLIP's visual manifold dramatically improves retrieval performance (+44 percentage points) while degrading text generation quality. This challenges the widely held assumption that stronger alignment universally benefits multimodal systems. The trade-off stems from information compression—alignment enhances cross-modal correspondences while sacrificing modality-specific acoustic details that lack direct visual counterparts. Our research contributions include both theoretical insights (Information Geometry Theory of Modal Manifolds) and practical tools (WhisperCLIP's multi-layer fusion architecture and AVE-2's five-dimensional alignment metrics), establishing a Pareto frontier where projected embeddings excel for retrieval tasks while raw embeddings deliver superior generation quality. This work advances multimodal systems toward seamless audio-visual integration while preserving each modality's distinctive characteristics.

