\textbf{Additive Construction}
The notions of \KC{} and \SA{} allow us to pose the question of designing \hg{} lattices as an additive construction problem by asking how to attach a new \cell{} to an existing \hg{} lattice (shown schematically in \figprefix\ref{fig:2_ADDITIVE}) such that the resulting structure remains both kinematically compatible, and collapsible. 

This corresponds to an iterative algorithm that additively builds an karigami lattice by adding scissors that satisfy \eqprefix{}\ref{eq:intrinsic_compatibility}, \ref{eq:collapsibility}.
This allows us for us to design the collapsed and deployed states simultaneously, ensuring that there is a continuous kinematic path between them.
We note that this deployment path generically passes through states of geometric incompatibility, analogous to those present when folding or unfolding origami \cite{Dudte2016-va, Choi2019-nr}.

