\paragraph{\textbf{Related Work}}

We now present related work on contrastive explanation within the broader context of logic-based explainable AI. It was originally for \emph{local explanation}, namely explaining why a classifier makes a certain decision for a \emph{given} input instance. 
As summarized in several works~\cite{ignatiev20,silva22,darwiche23}, local explanation answers one of the following two questions:
% \begin{quote}
     Q1. (Why): what minimal aspects of an instance guarantee the actual decision?
     Q2. (Why not): what minimal changes to an instance result in a different decision?
% \end{quote}
An answer to Q1 is nowadays commonly called a \emph{sufficient reason}
%\footnote{In \cite{barcelo2020model} it is called \emph{minimal sufficient reason}where sufficient reason has no minimality requirement. } 
\cite{DBLP:conf/ecai/DarwicheH20} or an AXp (short for abductive explanation) \cite{ignatiev2019abduction}. A sufficient reason is a minimal subset of its features' values s.t. changing any other feature values will not change the classification.
%In the Boolean classifier context, an instance is represented as a maximal consistent set of literals,
In $\PL$, a sufficient reason coincides with a \emph{prime implicant} of the Boolean classifier $\varphi$ which is \emph{locally true} in the given instance.
Therefore the concept was first introduced in the literature under the name PI-explanation \cite{shih2018formal}.
For Barcelo et al. \cite{barcelo2020model}, a sufficient reason itself need not be minimal, and it is called a \emph{minimal/minimum sufficient reason}, if it satisfies the subset-/cardinality-minimality requirement respectively.
We refer to these notions as instances of \emph{direct explanation}, for lack of a better term.

Dually, an answer to Q2 is commonly referred to as either a \emph{contrastive explanation} (CXp) \cite{ignatiev20}, a \emph{counterfactual explanation} \cite{stepin2021survey} or a necessary reason \cite{darwiche23}.
In all cases it is a subset-minimal part of the instance, changing the values of which results in a different classification. 
%There is a well-established duality result between AXps and CXps utilizing minimal hitting sets (MHS) \cite{ignatiev20}. 
The duality of AXp and CXp is well-established from a logical viewpoint via minimal hitting sets \cite{ignatiev20}.
Namely, an AXp is a subset-minimal intersection of every CXp of the instance and vice versa.
%A natural question rises: what about the global case?
%One may also enhance interest by shifting from subset-minimality to cardinality-minimality, that is, from minimal to minimum. The authors in \cite{barcelo2020model} propose problems called \emph{Minimum Sufficient Reason} and \emph{Minimum Change Required}. 
Similarly to before, a shift from subset-minimality to cardinality-minimality brings us to the notion of \emph{minimum change required} by Barcelo et al. \cite{barcelo2020model}.
%These are formulated as decision problems asking whether there exist explanations with size no larger than an input value $k$.
%The problems yield shortest explanations with size no larger than $k$, $k$ given as a part of the input.

We now move from local to global explanation.
%An interesting finding is that the aforementioned duality between direct and contrastive explanations does not extend globally -- at least not in the simplest way.
Note that an explanation can be called `global' in either \emph{conditional} or \emph{categorical} sense, namely either 1) it is a local explanation \emph{if an input instance satisfies it}, or 2) it explains the whole classifier.
%We notify them as global-c and global-a, respectively, for lack of better terms.
In the former sense, the duality between AXp and CXp extends naturally to \emph{global AXp} and \emph{counterexamples} \cite{silva22}. In propositional logic, they coincide with prime implicants and negated prime implicates.
An example of a global direct explanation in the latter sense is the (shortest) prime DNF expression of Boolean classifiers \cite{explainability1}.
%However, there is no straightforward correspondence of global-a contrastive explanation.
However, here one cannot obtain a corresponding contrastive explanation in terms of the duality, because the contrastive explanations referred to so far are by nature localized to the actual case, viz., they are counterfactual.
One must define a categorically global contrastive explanation in a genuinely global manner, which is a key contribution of our paper.

