\section{Preliminaries}

We will formulate our various explanation problems for an arbitrary logic. For the purposes of this work, a \textbf{logic} $\mathcal{L}$ is a tuple $L = (\mathcal{F}, \vDash, \sat)$, where $\mathcal{F}$ is a set of formulas, $\mathop{\vDash} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{F}) \times \mathcal{F}$ is a binary logical consequence relation and $\sat \subseteq \mathcal{F}$ is a unary satisfiability relation. Instead of $\varphi, \psi \in \mathcal{F}$ and $\{\varphi\} \vDash \psi$ we will write $\varphi, \psi \in \mathcal{L}$ and $\varphi \vDash \psi$ for simplicity. If for $S, S' \subseteq \mathcal{F}$ we have $S \vDash \psi$ for every $\psi \in S'$ and $S \vDash \varphi$ for every $\varphi \in S$, then we say that $S$ and $S'$ are equivalent, denoted $S \equiv S'$. We formulate our definitions for logics $\mathcal{L}$ with classical conjunction $\land$ and classical negation $\neg$ with the usual semantics. These connectives are not necessary but they help to keep our definitions clean and readable.
%We will also denote small sets of formulas such as $\{\varphi, \psi\} \subseteq \mathcal{F}$ by $\varphi \land \psi$ though our logic $\mathcal{L}$ may not have the conjunction connective.  \textcolor{red}{What bout negation, man?}


%For convenience, we will use the notation $\varphi \land \psi$ for the classical conjunction of $\varphi, \psi \in \mathcal{L}$ even when $\mathcal{L}$ does not have this connective. All of our definitions could be rewritten without this notation with less readable results. 

As the logics that we consider are two-valued, the above framework works best when modeling classifiers for binary classification. To model classifiers with more than two classes, we can assign to each class \(c\) a formula \(\varphi_c\) which is satisfied precisely by the inputs classified as \(c\). We call such a formula a \textbf{class formula}. This approach has been used, for example, in \cite{darwiche23}.

%The astute reader will note that the above framework for logics only really makes sense for binary truth values. For classifiers with multiple classes one can instead consider class formulas. For a classifier $C$ and a class $c$, a class formula $\varphi$ in some binary logic has the property that $s$ satisfies $\varphi$ iff $C(s) = c$ for all instances $s$. This approach has been used for example in \cite{darwiche23}.

%Though we formulate our definitions for arbitrary binary logics,
In addition to our very general definitions,
we will also consider the important special case of propositional logic. Let $\tau$ be a set of \textbf{proposition symbols} called a \textbf{vocabulary}. The set $\mathrm{PL}[\tau]$ of formulas of \textbf{propositional logic} over \(\tau\) is generated by the 
%following 
grammar
%\[
    $\varphi ::= \bot \mid p \mid \neg \varphi \mid \varphi \land \varphi \mid \varphi \lor \varphi$,
%\]
where $p \in \tau$. We define $\top := \neg \bot$ as an abbreviation. %We assume that all propositional formulas are in \textbf{negation normal form}, that is, negation only appears in literals. We discuss this choice further below when we define formula size.

A function $s : \sigma \to \{0,1\}$, where \(\sigma \subseteq \tau\), is called a \textbf{partial} \(\tau\)-\textbf{assignment}. When \(\sigma = \tau\), we say that \(s\) is a \(\tau\)-\textbf{assignment}. We often identify (partial) assignments with conjunctions of literals in the obvious way. The semantics of $\mathrm{PL}[\tau]$ is defined as usual, i.e., for a \(\tau\)-assignment \(s\) we define $s \nvDash \bot$ always, $s \vDash p$ if $s(p) = 1$, $s \vDash \neg \psi$ if $s \nvDash \psi$, $s \vDash \psi \land \theta$ if $s \vDash \psi$ and $s \vDash \theta$, and finally $s \vDash \psi \lor \theta$ if $s \vDash \psi$ or $s \vDash \theta$. Given \(S \subseteq \mathrm{PL}[\tau]\) and a (partial) \(\tau\)-assignment \(s\), we say that \(S\) \textbf{defines} \(s\), if \(S \equiv s\). 
If $S = \{\varphi\}$, we write $\varphi$ defines $s$ rather than $\{\varphi\}$ defines $s$.

Assignments that satisfy a formula are also called its \textbf{models}. For two formulas $\varphi, \psi \in \mathrm{PL}[\tau]$, we use the notation $\varphi \vDash \psi$ for \textbf{logical consequence}, that is $\varphi \vDash \psi$ if for all assignments $s$, $s \vDash \varphi$ implies $s \vDash \psi$. 
We extend this notation also to sets $S, T \subseteq \mathrm{PL}[\tau]$ of formulas, defining $S \vDash T$ if $\bigwedge S \vDash \bigwedge T$.

\begin{comment}
A function $s : \tau \to \{0,1\}$ is called an \textbf{assignment}. For the semantics of $\mathrm{PL}[\tau]$, we define when an assignment $s$ \textbf{satisfies} a formula $\varphi \in \mathrm{PL}[\tau]$, denoted $s \vDash \varphi$, as follows
\begin{itemize}
    \item $s \nvDash \bot$ always,
    \item $s \vDash p$ if $s(p) = 1$,
    \item $s \vDash \neg \psi$ if $s \nvDash \psi$,
    \item $s \vDash \psi \land \theta$ if $s \vDash \psi$ and $s \vDash \theta$,
    \item $s \vDash \psi \lor \theta$ if $s \vDash \psi$ or $s \vDash \theta$.
\end{itemize}
\end{comment}


%For a set $S \subseteq \mathrm{PL}[\tau]$ of formulas, the \textbf{theory of S}, denoted $\mathrm{Th}(S)$ is the set of logical consequences of $S$. That is, $\mathrm{Th}(S) = \{\varphi \in \mathrm{PL}[\tau] \mid S \vDash \varphi\}$.

Formulas of the form $p$ or $\neg p$, where $p$ is a proposition symbol, are called \textbf{literals}. The \textbf{dual} \(\overline{\ell}\) of a literal \(\ell\) is defined as $\overline{p} = \neg p$ and $\overline{\neg p} = p$. A disjunction of literals is called a \textbf{clause}. A formula $\varphi \in \mathrm{PL}[\tau]$ is in \textbf{conjunctive normal form}, or CNF, if $\varphi$ is a conjunction of clauses. We will often denote clauses as sets of literals and CNF-formulas as sets of clauses. For example, if a CNF-formula $\varphi$ has the clause $\neg p \lor q$, we might write $\{\neg p,q\} \in \varphi$. We denote the set of all CNF formulas $\varphi \in \mathrm{PL}[\tau]$ by $\mathrm{CNF}[\tau]$.
