%Throughout the paper, we will often refer to the size of formulas. 
%Intuitively, this refers to the length of the formula as a string of symbols, or the size of the syntax tree of the formula. 
Formula size is a key notion in our work. We do not define formula size in the general case as many different definitions could make sense depending on the logic considered. We will, however, consider the size of propositional formulas in practice so we define it here. We define the \textbf{size} of a formula $\varphi \in \mathrm{PL}[\tau]$ simply to be number of occurences of proposition symbols. More formally, we define it recursively as follows: $\size(\bot) = 0$ and $\size(p) = 1$, for a proposition symbol $p$; \(\size(\neg \psi) = \size(\psi)\); $\size(\psi_1 \land \psi_2) = \size(\psi_1 \lor \psi_2) = \size(\psi_1) + \size(\psi_2)$.
\begin{comment}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\size(\bot) = 0$ and $\size(p) = 1$, for a proposition symbol $p$.
    \item \(\size(\neg \psi) = \size(\psi)\).
    \item $\size(\psi_1 \land \psi_2) = \size(\psi_1 \lor \psi_2) = \size(\psi_1) + \size(\psi_2)$.
\end{itemize}
The choice of negation normal form, together with having all literals have size one, means that we consider positive and negative information, in the form of positive and negative literals, to be equal in terms of formula size. Between the literals $p$ and $\neg p$, neither should be preferred when minimizing formula size.

For our complexity characterisations, we are going to rely on the notion of \emph{witness oracles}~\cite{buss1993,janota2016} and their generalisation to the polynomial hierarchy~\cite{shen2019}. 
We recall that $\FPSigmaPlogwit{k}$ is the class of search problems for which some solution can be computed with logarithmically many calls to an $\Sigma^p_k$ oracle, $k\geq 1$, in polynomial time; here a witness oracle returns, on input of an instance of a problem in $\Sigma^p_k$, in case it is a yes-instance also a ``witness'', i.e., a polynomial size proof (or certificate) that can be checked with an $\Sigma^p_{k-1}$ oracle in polynomial time. In particular, for $k=1$ the class is $\FPNPlogwit$ and a witness oracle for SAT would return a satisfying assignment.
