\subsection{From Spatial Distances to Edge Weights} \label{cha:SpatialWeights}

% With the use of contiguity spatial weights spatial entities $i, j$ model the weights $w$ between them from the set {0, 1}. In case a spatial entity is a neighbor of another spatial entity, $w_{ij}$ is assigned 1. In case two spatial entities are not neighbors, $w_{i,j}$ is 0. A weight matrix could therefore be interpreted as an adjacency matrix between spatial entities. In order to link two different spatial entities, three different methods can be applied: Rook, Bishop and Queen linking. The difference in the linking strategies can be found in \autoref{fig:contiguity_weight_strategies}. While rook linking connects all neighbors if they share a vertex between the geometries, the bishop linking only considers links to be valid if they share an edge. In contrast, queen linking assigns 1 as a weight $w_{ij}$ in case a neighbor touches either an edge or a vertex. Contiguity based weighting is often used whenever neighborhood relations need to be determined between spatial geometries.

