\begin{remark}\label{rem:self-reduceTMcontainment}

 \iflong
Although the algorithm in Proposition~\ref{prop:TMcontainment} is for the decision version of the problem, a realization if one exists, can be computed using self-reducibility arguments as  follows. By iteratively deleting an edge of $G$ and running the algorithm of Proposition \ref{prop:TMcontainment}, we can identify a minimal subgraph $G'$ of $G$ that is a subdivision of $H$. Isolated vertices of $H$ can be mapped to isolated vertices of $G'$ in an arbitrary way. The number of vertices of $G'$ of degree at least three is bounded by $|V(H)|$, and the mapping of these to $V(H)$ can be guessed.  Moreover, the number of maximal paths in $G'$ that only have degree-2 vertices as internal vertices (call this set $\cal P$) must be bounded by a function of $|V(H)|$, and we can guess the mapping of vertices of $H$ of degree at most 2 into $\cal P$ and the final verification step is straightforward brute force. 
