  Answers to questions, even if they consist only of “ja”, “mhm” or “ok” were not annotated as \hrt, but as \change{} or as \hold{} if the speaker continued after the answer.
  {\hrt}s might also be followed by more talk by the interlocutor producing the \hrt \hspace{0.5mm} instead of the other interlocutor. This happens, for instance, if a speaker produces an acknowledgement token and then takes up the turn, often changing the (sub)topic.


\end{itemize}

