\begin{abstract}
The paper presents a strategy to construct an incremental Singular Value Decomposition (SVD) for time-evolving, spatially 3D discrete data sets. A low memory access  procedure for reducing and deploying the snapshot data is presented.
%which for example might refer to  spatially parallel computed PDE solutions is proposed.
Considered examples refer to Computational Fluid Dynamic (CFD) results extracted from unsteady flow simulations, which are computed spatially parallel using domain decomposition strategies and the Message Passing Interface (MPI) inter-processor communication protocol. The framework addresses state of the art PDE-solvers dedicated to practical applications. In particular, the spatial size of the data is assumed to be time-invariant and significantly larger than the temporal size due to the large computational mesh and the number of field quantities. 
%The overall procedure is transferable to other state-of-the-art fluid solvers \nkuehl{(e.g., OpenFOAM and its derivatives, Ansys Fluent/CFX, Siemens STAR CCM+, etc.)} and in general grid type/structure independent. 
Although the approach is applied to technical flows, it is applicable in similar applications under the umbrella of Computational Science and Engineering (CSE).
%, e.g., Computational Structure Dynamics (CSD), Acoustics (CA), or Electrodynamics (CEM).

The research highlights issues associated with large-scale simulations performed by spatially decomposing the data sets on distributed memory systems along the routes of a Single Instruction Multiple Data (SIMD) concept. Aspects relevant to the performance
%, such as --among others-- \trung{?appropriate pre- and postconditioning}, matrix-vector structures, and incremental adaptivity aspects, 
are scrutinized in detail. To this end, we introduce a bunch matrix that allows the aggregation of multiple time steps and 
%makes the incremental 
 SVD updates, and significantly
%after each time step obsolete.
%, resulting in a measurable increase in numerical efficiency. 
increases the computational efficiency.  



The incremental SVD strategy is initially verified and validated by simulating the 2D laminar single-phase flow around a circular cylinder.
%at Reynolds  $\mathrm{Re}_\mathrm{D} = 100$.  
Subsequent studies analyze the proposed strategy for a 2D submerged hydrofoil located %underneath traveling harmonic water waves
%at a depth related Froude number $\mathrm{Fn} = 0.62$ 
 in turbulent two-phase flows.
%for  $\mathrm{Re}_\mathrm{L} = 7.5 \cdot 10^5$.
Attention is directed to the 
%quality of the results
accuracy of the SVD-based reconstruction  
based on local and global flow quantities, their physical realizability, the independence of the domain partitioning, and related implementation aspects. Moreover, the influence of lower and (adaptive) upper construction rank thresholds on both the effort and the accuracy are assessed.
Finally, various pseudo-codes support the traceability and reprogramming. 

%Eventually, reduced memory requirements in terms of a truncated SVD and the associated increased computation times are discussed.
%
%concerning process adaptivity and truncated SVD evaluation realizability. 
% 
%Introducing a minimum SVD construction rank improves the adaptive behavior, which can become numerically uncomfortable due to the dominance of the first singular values. 
%In contrast, during the SVD evaluation process, inaccurate SVD reconstructions can violate fundamental physical constraints such as positive energies or volume concentrations and are, therefore, particularly considered.
%
 The incremental SVD process is applied to analyze and compress the predicted flow field around a Kriso container ship in harmonic head waves at $\mathrm{Fn} = 0.26$ and $\mathrm{Re}_\mathrm{L} = 1.4 \cdot 10^7$. With a numerical overhead of $\mathcal{O}(10\%)$, the snapshot matrix of size $\mathcal{O}( \mathbb{R}^{10E8  \times 10E4 } )$ computed on approximately 3000 processors can be incrementally compressed by $\mathcal{O}(95\%)$. The storage reduction is accompanied by errors in integral force and local wave elevation quantities of $\mathcal{O}(10^{-2}\%)$. 
\end{abstract}

