Since $\matr{C}$ refers to an augmented identity matrix, its QR decomposition follows a certain structure and supports simplifying the former rank-$b$ modification, i.e., $\matr{Q}_\mathrm{C} = \matr{I}$ and $\matr{R}_\mathrm{C} = \matr{C}$. Additionally, the zero entries in $\matr{C} = \begin{bmatrix} \matr{0} & \matr{I} \end{bmatrix}^\mathrm{T}$ yield $\begin{bmatrix} \matr{V} & \matr{0} \end{bmatrix}^\mathrm{T} \matr{C} = \matr{0}$, so that a $q$SVD 
