Eqns. (\ref{equ:update_V_column})-(\ref{equ:matrix_K_column}) serve as the basis for the on-the-fly or incrementally computed tSVD (itSVD) in this paper.

\paragraph{Important interim conclusion:} At each time step, the instantaneous state snapshot vector $\vect{y}$ is aligned as an additional column of the so-called bunch-matrix $\matr{B}$, and every $b$ time steps when $\matr{B}$ is filled, the latter is appended to the previous itSVD based on (\ref{equ:update_V_column}) - (\ref{equ:matrix_K_column}). Subsequently, $\matr{B}$ is cleared, and the process restarts. Hence, a trade-off between the bunch-size or column-size $b$ of $\matr{B}$, the overall itSVD construction time, and the required  memory arises. Two limit cases are conceivable: 
\begin{itemize} 
\item [(a)] The number of columns of the bunch matrix reaches the maximum number of time steps ($b \to \mathrm{T}$), 
