Over the last two decades, there has been an upsurge in research related to developing efficient routing protocols for wireless networks and IoT. Some works present enhancements in Dijkstra’s algorithm for routing \cite{5158843, 7435250}. However, such routing protocols are often associated with high local computations, storage requirements, and time complexities. Thus, it is inefficient to utilize them for resource-contained IoT networks today since they have to deliver quality services to users. Also, some other heuristic-based methods like sleep scheduling \cite{8481487}, fuzzy logic \cite{5158843}, trust-based or cluster-based approaches such as MANET, and VANET \cite{gomathy2020malicious, 7194748}, are also unable to meet the multiobjective expectations from IoT of the present and future. With the evolution of Machine Learning (ML), many ML-based approaches have been presented as promising solutions to routing-related optimization problems. Some of these routing algorithms are built on ML paradigms such as clustering \cite{gomathy2020malicious}, and evolutionary algorithms \cite{10516314}. 


RL, among other data-driven ML approaches, has attracted special attention since it provides us with the facility to learn optimal policies while interacting with the environment. Thus, many RL-based routing protocols have been proposed \cite{10419073, 10608117, 8935210, 9504585, abadi2022rlbeep, bouzid2020efficient, guo2019optimizing, 9819969, 9171590, 9634122, 9488736, liu2021drl, chen2021multiagent, rischke2020qr, cong2021deep, ergun2022reinforcement, 9685191, chen2020rl, 10012574, huang2022intelligent, phan2022deepair}.
\iffalse
Certain RL-based routing protocols have been proposed for specific applications. As an instance, Malik et al. \cite{malik2022rl} present an RL-based routing scheme for Cognitive Radio Network (CRN) based IoT. A Q-Learning-based approach is used in addition to no-external regret learning-based channel selection. Thus, multiple objectives, such as data rate, packet collision rate, and end-to-end delay, are co-optimized. In \cite{8839827}, a combination of graph routing algorithm and Q-learning is presented for latency and lifetime enhancements in Industrial Wireless Sensor Networks. Xinge Li et al. \cite{9076600} propose a Multi-Agent RL-based routing protocol for underwater optical WSNs. They enhance the traditional q-learning approach with two optimization schemes: Position-Based Q-Value Initialization and Variant Learning Rate. They use the weighted sum of multiple objectives for reward formulation. The enhancements to Q-learning they've presented may be utilized for other IoT applications. However, no such discussion has been made about their possibilities and associated challenges. They also have some drawbacks, like centralized decision-making and lack of adaptability with dynamically changing preferences in the network. We explain them in more detail below.
