 In the context of routing, the state space is given by:
$$\mathcal{S}=\mathcal{N}\times\mathcal{N}\cup \{s_T\}.$$
Here, each state $(i,j)\in \mathcal{N}\times\mathcal{N}$ indicates that the packet is currently at node $i$ with the destination being node $j$.  The actions space $\mathcal{A}$ corresponds to the possible decisions or routing choices available at each state. In particular, for state $s=(i,j)\in \mathcal{N}\times\mathcal{N} $  the action space is
$$\mathcal{A}(s)=\{i'\in \mathcal{N}| (i,i')\in \mathcal{E}\}$$
where $a\in \mathcal{A}(s)$ represents selecting the next node $a\in \mathcal{N}$ to which the packet should be forwarded from node $i$ on the path to the destination $j$. The terminal state $s_T$ has no actions. The transition function for a state $s=(i,j)$ where $i\neq j$ is
$$T(s,a)=\begin{cases} (a,j) & \text{ with probability } 1-P(i,a) \\ s_T &\text{ with probability } P(i,a)  \end{cases}$$
 and if $i=j$ it is simply $T(s,a)=s_T$.


