\subsection{Markov Decision Process (MDP)}

 Routing is naturally formulated as finite state Markov Decision Process (MDP)~\cite{book}. MDP consists of states $\mathcal{S}$, actions $\mathcal{A}$, and a reward function. In the context of routing, the state space is given by:
$$\mathcal{S}=\mathcal{N}\times\mathcal{N}\cup \{T\}.$$
Here, each state $(i,j)\in \mathcal{N}\times\mathcal{N}$ indicates that the packet is currently at node $i$ with the destination being node $j$. The state $T$ represents a terminal state for dropped packets, indicating that the packet has been lost and will not reach its destination. The actions corresponds to the possible decisions or routing choices available at each state. In particular, for state $s=(i,j)\in \mathcal{N}\times\mathcal{N} $  the action space is
$$\mathcal{A}(s)=\{i'\in \mathcal{N}| (i,i')\in \mathcal{E}\}$$
where $a\in \mathcal{A}(s)$ represents selecting the next node $a\in \mathcal{N}$ to which the packet should be forwarded from node on the path to the destination $j$. The terminal state $T$ has no actions.










 We consider the episodic routing MDP, where a source node sends a packet to a destination node in each episode. During routing, at each step, the packet moves from one node to the next node. We thus propose the following state, action, and reward formulations for the MDP:

\textbf{State}: The tuple $\langle$ Current node ID, Destination node ID $\rangle$ is the state of the environment. The current node is the node in which the packet is currently present. The state space is thus defined as $\mathcal{S} = \{  (n, m) \mid n, m \in \mathcal{N}\}$.

% If the destination node varies between packets, the state space $\mathcal{S}$ for the given MDP will be the same as $\mathcal{N}$. However, for the scenario with a constant destination node for all packets, say a base station, each local Q-table will have only one state (and thus one row). In such a case, the node-id itself acts as the state. 

\textbf{Action}: When the packet is present at a node $i\in \mathcal{N}$, the action is to choose one of the current node's neighbors as the next relay node. Thus, the action space is defined as $ \mathcal{A}(i) = \{\mathcal{N}(i) \mid \text{where } \mathcal{N}(i) \subset \mathcal{N} \text{ is the set of neighbours of node } i\in \mathcal{N}\}$.


\textbf{Reward} 
  Each action the RL agent takes results in a reward from the environment, which indicates how good the chosen action is. Thus, the reward formulation should capture the objectives we want to optimize. The operating environment and the characteristics of the underlying network influence the objectives of IoT networks. These objectives can vary widely based on the type of network (e.g., wired, wireless, mobile, ad-hoc), the application's requirements (e.g., latency, bandwidth, reliability), and the specific operating conditions (e.g., network topology, node mobility, energy constraints). We focus on two vital objectives common to all IoT networks: packet delivery and energy efficiency. Thus, the reward that the agent receives from the environment at an episode $k$ while forwarding a packet from node $i$ to node $i'$ has two component rewards:

 \begin{enumerate}
     \item \textbf{Energy consumption}, $E(i,i')$:
     \begin{align}\label{eq:TransmissionEnergy}
     E(i,i') = e^t(i, i') {+} e^r(i')
     \end{align}

      where $e^t(i, i')$ is the energy consumed for transmitting a packet from node $i$  and $e^r(i')$ the energy consumed for receiving it at node $i'$. Appendix \ref{subsec:energyCost} presents the details of the energy consumption model.

     \item \textbf{Delivery reward}, $D(i, i')$:
     \begin{equation}
  D(i, i') = \begin{cases}
        1,  & \text{If the packet is delivered to destination}
        \\
        0, & \text{Otherwise}.
        \end{cases}
 \end{equation}
 \end{enumerate}   

In MORL, the various components of reward, representing the different objectives, are often converted into a scalarized reward by taking a weighted sum of the components. Thus, the total reward at an episode $k$ is the convex combination between the two components:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:reward}
    R_k(i,i') \gets (1-\beta_k)D(i,i') - \beta_k E(i,i')
\end{equation}
 Here, the weighting factor between the two objectives is $\beta_k \in [0, 1]$.


