\section{Distributed Q-Learning for IoT Routing}
\label{sec:distributedQLearn}



We propose a novel distributed Q-Learning approach for routing in IoT scenarios where the nodes can not or prefer not to share their data with a global controller. Rather, they can learn intelligent routing by sharing limited information with the immediate neighboring nodes. Moreover, it requires only a local version of small Q-tables stored at each node, as opposed to huge global Q-tables in centralized approaches. Q-learning is a model-free reinforcement learning algorithm ~\cite{sutton1999reinforcement}. At a time step $k$, the agent observes the state $s_k \in S$ of the environment, takes an action $a_k \in A$, and observes the reward $R_k$ it receives from the environment. The state of the environment changes to  $s_{k+1} \in S$ after the agent takes the action. The main goal of Q-learning is to learn the best actions to take in a given state to maximize the cumulative reward over time. For this purpose, the RL agent maintains a Q-table table or a Q-value function $Q(s, a)$. The value $Q(s, a)$ represents the expected cumulative reward starting from state $s$, taking an action $a$, and following the optimal policy thereafter. An iterative process is followed to estimate the Q-values. The Q-table is initialized with arbitrary values (often zeros). As the agent interacts with the environment and gathers experience, the Q-values are iteratively updated using the following Q-learning update rule:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:q_update}
Q(s_k, a_k) = (1-\alpha) Q(s_k, a_k) {+} \alpha[ R_k {+} \gamma \max Q(s_{k+1}, a_{k+1})]
\end{equation}

where $\alpha$ is the learning rate controlling how much the new information influences the Q-value. and $\gamma\in [0,1]$ is the discount factor controlling how much the future rewards influence the Q-value. $\max Q(s_{k+1}, a_{k+1})$ is the maximum Q-value for the next state $s_{k+1}$ for all possible actions $a_{k+1}$. 


We observe from equations \ref{eq:reward} and \ref{eq:q_update} that to calculate $R_k(i, i')$, and $Q(i, i')$, the transmitting node $i$ needs various values that are private to the receiving node $i'$. This interdependence between nodes for optimal routing decisions seems to be the prominent reason for choosing centralized protocols where a central controller can access all the nodes' information. However, since the problem at hand follows Markov's property, Q-table update at node $i$ only depends on the node $i'$, not other nodes. Thus, in the proposed distributed approach, Fig. \ref{fig:distributed-Q-Learn}, after receiving a packet from node $i$, node $i'$ sends an acknowledgment packet back with all the information the node $i$ needs to update its Q-table. The proposed acknowledgment packet structure thus contains the following information: maximum Q-value in the table of node $i'$ ($\max Q(i', .)$), the energy consumed in receiving the packet ($e^r(i')$), and the delivery reward $D(i, i')$. The node $i$, after receiving the acknowledgment packet, updates its local Q-table according to the equation \ref{eq:q_update}. Thus, each node can make autonomous decisions based on the local Multi-Objective Q-learning policy with negligible additional communication cost and minimum data sharing.
% Packet structure inspired by \cite{9076600}


\section{Multiobjective-Q-Learning-based Dynamic and Distributed Routing (\texttt{MOQLDDR})}


\subsection{Multiobjective-Q-Learning for Dynamic Routing}

\begin{figure}[t]%
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{img/Diagrams - Pareto front.png}
\caption{Pareto front in Multiobjective Optimization }\label{fig:Pareto-Front.jpg}
\end{figure}
In single-objective RL problems, there is a unique optimal value, though multiple optimal policies may exist that yield this value. The primary objective in single-objective RL is to learn one of these optimal policies. However, in the multi-objective scenario, without additional information regarding the user’s preference vector, it is impossible to find a single solution that would be optimal
for all the objectives simultaneously. In such cases, multiple potentially optimal values can arise. In this set of optimal solutions, enhancing the total reward for one objective is impossible without diminishing the reward for another \cite{Vaishnav2024}. Each solution with this characteristic is called Pareto efficient, collectively forming the Pareto frontier; see Figure \ref{fig:Pareto-Front.jpg}. MORL aims to find the set of policies that yield the Pareto front.

 Mathematically, consider $X\subseteq \mathbb {R} ^{n}$ as the set of feasible solutions for the aforementioned optimization problem presented in eq \ref{eq_moop} with the two objectives, namely \texttt{Energy} and \texttt{PDR}. We aim to maximize the \texttt{PDR} and minimize \texttt{Energy}. For the problem under consideration, a feasible solution $x_1 \in X$ is said to (Pareto) dominate another solution $x_2 \in X$ if 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\texttt{Energy}(x_1) \leq \texttt{Energy}(x_2)$, and  $\texttt{PDR}(x_1) \geq \texttt{PDR}(x_2)$
    \item $\texttt{Energy}(x_1) < \texttt{Energy}(x_2)$, and/or $\texttt{PDR}(x_1) > \texttt{PDR}(x_2)$
\end{enumerate}


A solution $x^* \in X$ and the corresponding value $f(x^*)$ is considered Pareto optimal if another solution does not exist that dominates it. The set of Pareto optimal solutions, denoted 
by $X^*$ is called the Pareto front, Pareto frontier, or Pareto boundary. Constructing a Pareto front is possible by finding the optimal solutions for different tradeoffs between the objectives. The tradeoffs can be mathematically visualized using a weight vector or preference vector. The preference vector indicates the relative weightage given to each objective in a particular tradeoff. In our routing scenario (eqn \ref{eq:reward}), the vector [$\beta$, $1-\beta$] is the preference vector of the considered multi-objective Q-learning approach. The number of objectives in the MORL problem determines the length of the preference vector. Thus, we have a preference vector of length two in this work. In the proposed \texttt{MOQLDDR} approach, we first select a set of preference vectors to help approximate the entire Pareto front. We select this set through a uniform grid search process. We call the set of these preference vectors the training preference set, $\mathcal{P}$. 
 We propose to approximate and utilize the Pareto front in the following steps:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Exploration and Training:} Exploring the environment and training one Q-table corresponding to each preference vector in $\mathcal{P}$, thus learning the optimal policies for all the chosen tradeoffs (assuming sufficient exploration of states and actions).
    \item \textbf{Selection:} Selecting the appropriate policy (trained Q-table) that should be utilized for routing decisions to capture the user's or decision-maker's current preference.
    \item \textbf{Execution:} Executing the chosen policy (Q-table) for routing. 
\end{enumerate}


