The experiments are conducted on 11th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-1185G7 CPU @ 3.00GHz with 32 GB RAM machine with Windows operating system. Experiments use MAC protocols as IEEE 802.15.4, in which the packet size is assumed as $133$ Bytes and the communication overhead per packet is considered as $30$ Bytes. Further, the simulation is operated using a network of $100$ IoT nodes uniformly deployed in a $100 m^2$ field. During evaluations,  various parameters such as $\alpha$, $E_{idle}$, $N$, and $E_{awake}$ are defined as 0.9, 0.5 nJ, 100, and 0.5$\mu J$, respectively. %We conduct four experiments. 
In each simulation, we consider four sets of experiments run five times each under two different scenarios, such as sequential (Experiment 1 \& 2) and simultaneous (Experiment 3 \& 4) exploration strategies, by varying their frequency of preference variations periodically (every 1000th episode) and frequently (each episode). 

The variation of $\epsilon$-values and  preference variation are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:Exp_setting_dynamic_online}. Since experiments 1 and 2  are characterized by sequential exploration and exploitation, the $\epsilon$ value (Figure~\ref{fig:epsilonVariation1}) in the proposed algorithm is $1$ in the first $1000$ episodes (indicating full exploration and no exploitation) and $0$ afterward (indicating no exploration and only exploitation). In experiments $3$ and $4$ (Figure~\ref{fig:epsilonVariation2}), the $\epsilon$ is set as $1$ in the beginning and then linearly reduced to $0$, thus achieving the desired simultaneous exploration and exploitation. In all the experiments, the $\epsilon$ values for the \texttt{SMORLR} baseline are reset to $1$ each time the decision-maker changes the preference. Then, it linearly reduces to $0$, encouraging fresh exploration after each preference-change. The frequency of preference variation is a thousand episodes in experiments $1$ and $3$ (Figure~\ref{fig:preferenceVariation1}), and each episode in experiments $2$ and $4$ (Figure~\ref{fig:preferenceVariation2}). The two different preference variation patterns are considered to study the effect of the frequency of preference changes on performance. 

