Figure \ref{fig:online_total_packets_delivered.png} provides a more comprehensive view by showcasing cumulative packet delivery, which offers a fair comparison over the long run. The proposed method starts outperforming all baselines, after $3000$ episodes of sufficient learning. As time progresses, the performance gap widens. By the end of $10,000$ episodes, the proposed method delivers $46.67\%$ more packets than \texttt{RSPAR}, $69.23\%$ more packets than \texttt{SMORLR}, $164\%$ more packets than \texttt{FMOLD}, $230\%$ more packets than \texttt{RLBR}, and $255.12\%$ more packets than \texttt{R2LTO} and \texttt{RLBEEP}. Similar results were seen in Experiment 4, where preferences were randomly changed in each episode. As depicted in Figure \ref{fig:delivery_online_cont_change}, the total number of packets delivered by the proposed routing protocol consistently and significantly exceeds those of the baselines, with the gap widening as more episodes are completed. Specifically, at the end of $5000$ episodes, the total packet delivery for the proposed algorithm reaches around $3200$, which is $33.33\%$ more than \texttt{RSPAR}, around $166\%$ more than \texttt{FMOLD} and \texttt{RLBR}, $220\%$ more than \texttt{SMORLR}, and $814.3\%$ more than more than \texttt{RLBEEP} and \texttt{R2LTO}. The proposed method outperforms all baselines when the preference for \texttt{PDR} objective is higher, and is also acceptable for preferences at lower levels. 

\section{Conclusions} \label{sec:conclusion}
This paper presents a dynamic and distributed IoT routing protocol based on multi-objective Q-learning, designed to adapt to changing priorities in real-time. This approach optimizes key metrics like overall reward, energy efficiency and PDR. By leveraging distributed learning at individual nodes, the method avoids the need for centralized control, enhancing scalability and robustness. Simulations show that this approach outperforms existing algorithms in adaptability, energy efficiency, and packet delivery. The proposed approach shows consistent out-performance irrespective of the exploration strategies, and preference change patterns. The study highlights the potential of reinforcement learning for dynamic IoT networks and suggests future work on extending the algorithm to address security and more complex objectives. This makes it a promising solution for real-time, flexible routing in evolving IoT environments.




