  \subsection{Energy Cost Model}
  \label{subsec:energyCost}
    We now specify how we compute the energy of the routing path $E(i,j)$. As used in \cite{9504585}, we consider the radio energy consumption model \cite{926982}. In this model, there are two
types of channels used to calculate path loss. Based on the distance between the transmitting and receiving nodes $d$, the two models used are (1) free space model (when $d \le d_0$) and (2) multipath fading model (when $d>d_0$). Here $d_0$ is the distance threshold calculated as:
\begin{align}\label{eq:distanceThreshold}
    d_0 = \sqrt{\frac{E_{fs}}{E_{mp}}}
\end{align}
where $E_{fs}$ is the energy consumption of free space, and $E_{mp}$ is the energy consumption of multipath.

 The energy consumed for transmitting a packet from node $a$ and $b$ is given by:
 \begin{equation}
  e^t_{a, b} = \begin{cases}
        m \ast (E_{pr} {+} d^2 \ast E_{fs}),  & \text{ $d \le d_0$}
        \\
        m \ast (E_{pr} {+} d^4 \ast E_{mp}),  & \text{ $d > d_0$}.
        \end{cases}
 \end{equation}
 where $m$ is the size (in bytes) of the data packet being transmitted, $E_{pr}$ be the energy consumption
caused by the circuit processing before the transmission of data
packet, and after the reception of a data packet. The energy consumed for receiving the packet at node $b$ is given by:
    \begin{equation}
        e^r_{b} = m \ast E_{pr}
    \end{equation}
